# Semantic Commit Messages

After completing changes to source code or documentation, **always suggest a semantic commit message** — even when the user did not ask to commit.

## Format

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(<scope>): <short imperative summary>
```

- **type**: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `perf`, `style`
- **scope** (optional): affected area, e.g. `climate`, `editor`, `registry`, `room-view`
- **summary**: imperative mood, lowercase, no trailing period, max ~72 characters

## When to Suggest

Suggest a commit message when you:
- Implement a feature or fix
- Refactor or restructure code
- Update documentation (`CLAUDE.md`, `README`, comments with behavioral impact)
- Change build, CI, or release tooling

Do **not** suggest a commit unless you actually made or described concrete changes.

## Examples (match this repo's style)

```
feat(climate): support Better Thermostat UI card (normal/mini)
fix: exclude Tankerkoenig binary sensors from security view and summary
docs: clarify temp/humidity sensor assignment is in HA settings
refactor: editor Lit migration with HA-styled UI and entity search
chore: bump version to v1.3.4-beta.8
```

## How to Present

At the end of your response, include a short **Suggested commit** block:

```
Suggested commit:
feat(scope): concise description of why the change was made
```

- Focus on **why**, not a file list
- Add `Closes #NN` or `(#NN)` when the change addresses a known issue or PR
- If changes span multiple unrelated concerns, note that they should be split into separate commits

## Constraints

- Suggesting a message does **not** mean committing — only commit when the user explicitly asks
- One logical change → one suggested message; do not batch unrelated work into a single message
